                <pb facs="0946" n="810" sameAs="frus:frus1888p1/pg810"/>

                <head>No. 583.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Bayard to Mr. Edwardes.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1888-06-08"><hi rendition="#italic">June</hi> 8,
                        1888</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Referring to your note of yesterday and my
                    reply of this morning in relation to the information received by Lord Salisbury,
                    to the effect that the consular officer of the United States at St. Pierre had
                    instigated breaches of the Newfoundland bait laws, I beg leave to inform you
                    that I have just received a telegram from the officer referred to, denying such
                    charge and stating his advice to American fishing vessels to comply faithfully
                    with the local laws.</p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">T. F. Bayard.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
